# ============================================================
#  Git Commit Message Template
#  Project: <Project Name>
#  Usage: Loaded by `git commit` once commit.template points
#         here. Wire it up with `/repo-init`, or manually:
#         git config commit.template .gitmessage
# ============================================================
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Commit Message Format
# ------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   <type>(<scope>): <subject>
#   <BLANK LINE>
#   <body>
#   <BLANK LINE>
#   <footer>
#
#   Header  - single line, imperative mood, no trailing period;
#             subject within 50 characters when possible, hard
#             limit 72 characters.
#   Body    - one bullet per logical change, prefixed "- ";
#             each bullet stays on a SINGLE physical line (no
#             wrapping inside a bullet) and explains the why
#             when non-obvious. A blank line separates the
#             body from the header and footer.
#   Footer  - machine-parseable metadata such as issue links,
#             breaking change notices, and authorship tags.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Commit Types
# ------------------------------------------------------------
#   feat      - Introduce a user-visible capability.
#   fix       - Correct an incorrect or broken behavior.
#   docs      - Update documentation or in-code comments only.
#   style     - Adjust formatting without logic impact.
#   refactor  - Restructure code without altering behavior.
#   perf      - Improve runtime, memory, or throughput.
#   test      - Add or revise automated test assets.
#   build     - Modify build system, packaging, or toolchain.
#   ci        - Adjust continuous integration or delivery.
#   chore     - Routine maintenance with no functional impact.
#   revert    - Roll back a previously merged commit.
#   merge     - Record a non-trivial branch integration
#               (non-fast-forward merge commits only).
#   deps      - Bump or pin third-party dependencies.
#   config    - Modify configuration files or default values.
#   env       - Update environment setup or provisioning.
#   ui        - Modify user-facing presentation or layout.
#   security  - Address vulnerabilities or harden defenses.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Scope
# ------------------------------------------------------------
#   - Scope is optional but recommended when the change is
#     bounded to a known module, package, directory, or layer.
#   - Prefer the most stable identifier available: top-level
#     package name, directory name, or public component name.
#   - Use a single scope per commit; split the commit when the
#     change legitimately spans multiple scopes.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Footer Tags
# ------------------------------------------------------------
#   Closes #<issue-id>      close referenced issue on merge (GitHub/GitLab style).
#   Fixes #<issue-id>       alias for Closes.
#   Refs #<issue-id>        reference without closing.
#   BREAKING CHANGE: <msg>  flag incompatible interface change.
#   Co-authored-by: <name> <email>   (human collaborators
#                           only; never an AI, agent, or
#                           assistant attribution)
#
# ============================================================
# Branch Management
# ============================================================
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Workflow Variant
# ------------------------------------------------------------
#   Adopt exactly one variant per repository, the same way a
#   naming variant is adopted below, and record it on the
#   `Workflow:` line. Tooling reads that line to decide which
#   branches are protected and whether committing directly on
#   a trunk is allowed.
#
#   Workflow: <not recorded>
#
#   git-flow     - `main` plus `develop` trunks; all work lands
#                  through short-lived branches and a reviewed
#                  PR or MR. Protected: `main`, `develop`,
#                  `support/*`. Default for team repositories.
#   github-flow  - Single `main` trunk; work lands through
#                  short-lived branches and a PR or MR. No
#                  `develop`. Protected: `main`, `support/*`.
#   trunk-solo   - Single `main` trunk; committing directly on
#                  `main` is the normal path and branches are
#                  optional. No `develop`. Protected: none.
#                  Intended for single-maintainer repositories.
#
#   Force push and history rewrite stay forbidden on `main`,
#   `develop`, and `support/*` in EVERY variant, including
#   `trunk-solo` where those branches are otherwise open.
#
#   When the `Workflow:` line is absent or still reads
#   `<not recorded>`, tooling assumes `git-flow` (the
#   strictest variant) and asks which one applies; record the
#   answer here so the question does not return. Tooling must not guess from history shape: a
#   squash-merged PR flow and a direct-commit trunk produce
#   the same linear topology.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Branch Types
# ------------------------------------------------------------
#   main       - Production-ready, release-tagged trunk.
#                Protected (see Protected Branches below).
#   develop    - Integration trunk for the next release cycle.
#                Receives merges from `feature/*` and
#                `bugfix/*` branches. Protected.
#   feature/*  - Net-new functionality scoped to a single
#                concern. Branched from `develop`, merged
#                back into `develop`, then deleted.
#   bugfix/*   - Non-urgent defect fix targeting the next
#                release. Branched from `develop`, merged
#                back into `develop`, then deleted.
#   release/*  - Release stabilization and final adjustments.
#                Branched from `develop`, merged into `main`
#                with a release tag, then back-merged into
#                `develop`.
#   hotfix/*   - Urgent fix against the production line.
#                Branched from `main`, merged into `main`
#                with a patch tag, then back-merged into
#                `develop` and any active `release/*` branch.
#   support/*  - Long-lived maintenance line for a previous
#                major version. Branched from the matching
#                release tag on `main`; kept alive as long as
#                that major version is supported.
#
#   The list above describes `git-flow`. Under `github-flow`
#   and `trunk-solo` there is no `develop`: `feature/*` and
#   `bugfix/*` branch from `main` and merge back into `main`,
#   and `release/*` stabilizes on `main` directly. Under
#   `trunk-solo` every short-lived type stays available but is
#   optional rather than mandatory.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Branch Naming
# ------------------------------------------------------------
#   The variants below apply to short-lived branches only.
#   Long-lived branches `main`, `develop`, and `support/*`
#   use their fixed names without a `<type>/` prefix.
#
#   Adopt exactly one of the following variants per repository
#   and apply it consistently across all contributors.
#
#   Variant A : <type>/<issue-id>-<short-desc>
#               Use when an issue tracker is the source of
#               truth; the id keeps branches traceable.
#               `<issue-id>` follows the tracker's native
#               format (numeric id, or prefix plus numeric id).
#
#   Variant B : <type>/<scope>/<short-desc>
#               Use when work is organized by module or
#               package and naturally aligns with commit
#               scope.
#
#   Variant C : <type>/<short-desc>
#               Use when issue tracking is informal and PR
#               titles already carry the necessary context.
#
#   Record the adopted variant on the line below once decided
#   (`/repo-init` maintains it; tooling reads it):
#   Adopted: <not recorded>
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Naming Character Rules
# ------------------------------------------------------------
#   - Use lowercase ASCII letters, digits, and `-` only.
#   - Use `/` strictly as the type or scope separator.
#   - Join words inside `<short-desc>` with `-` (hyphen).
#   - Do not use spaces, underscores, uppercase, accented
#     letters, or non-ASCII glyphs.
#   - Keep total branch name length within 60 characters to
#     remain readable in tools and CI logs.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Protected Branches
# ------------------------------------------------------------
#   The protected set follows the active workflow variant:
#     git-flow    -> `main`, `develop`, `support/*`
#     github-flow -> `main`, `support/*`
#     trunk-solo  -> none
#
#   - A protected branch forbids direct commit and direct push;
#     updates land only via PR or MR.
#   - Every PR or MR against a protected branch requires at
#     least one approving review and a green CI status before
#     merge.
#   - Force push and history rewrite are forbidden on `main`,
#     `develop`, and `support/*` under EVERY variant, including
#     `trunk-solo`, where direct commits are allowed but
#     rewriting published history still is not.
#   - Force push is permitted only on personal short-lived
#     branches (`feature/*`, `bugfix/*`, `hotfix/*`, and
#     `release/*` before its first release tag).
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Tags
# ------------------------------------------------------------
#   - Annotated tags only (`git tag -a`); lightweight tags are
#     local bookmarks and never pushed.
#   - Release and patch tags use `v<major>.<minor>.<patch>`
#     (pre-release suffixes like `-rc.1` are allowed) and sit
#     on `main` at the release/* or hotfix/* merge commit.
#   - Never move, delete, or force-push a published tag; ship
#     a new version instead.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# PR/MR and Commit Linkage
# ------------------------------------------------------------
#   - PR or MR title MUST follow the commit header format:
#       <type>(<scope>): <subject>
#   - With `squash merge`, the PR or MR title becomes the
#     final commit subject and the description becomes the
#     final commit body; verify both against this template
#     before approving the merge.
#   - With `merge commit`, every contained commit must
#     already satisfy this template.
#   - Reference issues in the PR or MR description with
#     `Closes #<issue-id>`, `Fixes #<issue-id>`, or
#     `Refs #<issue-id>` so that issue state stays consistent
#     with branch history.
#
# ============================================================
# Skeleton (kept commented so saving an unedited buffer aborts
# the commit instead of landing literal placeholders; write the
# real message at the top of this buffer)
# ============================================================
#   <type>(<scope>): <subject>
#
#   <body>
#
#   Closes #<issue-id>
# ============================================================
